# gitleaks' generic-api-key rule fires on a lowercase identifier with digits
# next to the word `key`. The v1 build wrote its browser-storage keys with
# DOTS -- `spicystock.trades.v2`, `spicystock.trade-journal.v1` -- assigned to
# `KEY` and `LEGACY_KEY`, so `localStorage.getItem` arguments read to the
# scanner as credentials. Every one of the fourteen findings below is such a
# key: read at the commit named, in the four files the v2 rewrite deleted in
# 01c000d. Nothing here was ever a secret, and nothing here is on `main`.
#
# Each line is ONE finding: <commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>. It suppresses that
# line of that immutable historical commit and nothing else -- not the file,
# not the value, not the rule. Proved: re-introducing the same storage key in
# a NEW commit is flagged again, and planted credential-shaped values are
# still found in these very paths. Never add a line here for a finding you
# have not read at its commit and shown is not a secret.
#
# The current code writes these keys with COLONS (`spicystock:following:v1`)
# for the same reason; see CLAUDE.md's note on the scan.

